</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F3.sf4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">(d)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Solvers and time schedules.</span>
We compare <span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">AdaSDE</span> against state-of-the-art single-step and multi-step ODE solvers. The single-step baselines include training-free methods—DDIM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>, EDM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, and DPM-Solver-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, as well as the lightweight-tuning approach AMED-Solver <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. For multi-step methods, we evaluate DPM-Solver++ (3M) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, UniPC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, and iPNDM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>. To further demonstrate the effectiveness of our method, we also conduct a head-to-head comparison between DPM-Plugin and DPM-Solver-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
